    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Leapmotor B03 Arrives in the UK, It Will Start at Under £25,000\nMechanic Inspects a 250,000-Mile Toyota Pickup Truck and This Is What He Finds\n17 photos\nPhoto: Car Wizard | YouTube\nThe owner took his 1993 Toyota Pickup at a service shop for a milestone checkup. The vehicle has recently hit 250,000 miles at the respectable age of 33 years old. The mechanic has already informed the customer that he won't let it drive out of his shop before he checks everything about it. However, the owner refused to fix all the issues that he found.\nToyota Pickup is not just a simple way of referring to this model. That is exactly how Toyota officially named it in the United States and Canada, while it was sold as the Toyota Hilux in most other international markets. Toyota also introduced a larger, full-size truck model in 1993, called the T100.\nTypical of Toyota, the fifth-generation compact trucks built by the Japanese firm between 1989 and 1995 are widely known for bombproof reliability, blocky early 1990s design, and mechanical simplicity. And yes, everything is mechanical and manual on the truck that just drove into the shop belonging to David Long, a.k.a. The Car Wizard.\nIt has power, nothing. It even comes with crank windows, just like Slate’s new electric truck, and it doesn't even have a single airbag on board. This truck rolled off the production line before such safety features became mandatory. Frontal airbags became mandatory for all new passenger cars and light trucks in the United States starting with the 1999 model year.\nPhoto: Car Wizard | YouTube Back in the day, Toyota offered two engine options, so customers could go home with either the 2.4-liter inline-four, which produced 116 horsepower and 140 pound-feet of torque, or the 3.0-liter V6 engine, as this truck has.\nThe V6 pushes out 150 horsepower (152 metric horsepower) and 180 pound-feet (244 Newton meters) of torque. A five-speed manual transmission puts the power down through all four corners. The truck can tow up to 3,500 pounds (1,588 kilograms). But as bulletproof as the Toyota Pickup may have been in its era, time is catching up with it. It did age like fine wine, but it aged. It's only natural.\nThe last time the Toyota pickup truck went through proper, thorough maintenance work was back in 1997 when it had 103,000 miles (165,762 kilometers) on the clock. That is nearly 30 years and 150,000 miles (241,402 kilometers) ago.\nThe mechanic is now desperately trying to make up for the lost time. That is if the owner will let him. But sourcing parts for a truck that old has been a massive challenge, because he couldn’t find a single shop out there that has all the parts.\nPhoto: Car Wizard | YouTube Besides, the Wizard has a long list of issues that he has to deal with. The air conditioning is not working, and the owner doesn’t even consider fixing it or finding out why it doesn’t work, because he doesn’t want to pay for the procedure. So this is as far as the mechanical investigation goes.\nThe all-wheel-drive system doesn't work either, and the conversation between the mechanic and the owner stopped with \" I don't want it fixed .\" Furthermore, the power steering fluid has turned brown, the timing cover has oil leaking all over, and the timing belt is nearly 30 years old. When he gets that apart, he will figure out the source of the leak.\nThe Wizard already installed a new distributor, ignition, spark plugs, and wires. The fuel filter also needs to be changed because it has been eaten by rust. The rear differential is leaking, and the transmission and transfer case are due for a fluid change as well.\nThe truck no longer has a windshield washer system. It didn’t simply vanish over the years. The owner admitted that he was in the midst of doing some repairs, probably in his own garage or driveway, and will put it back in when he's done.\nPhoto: Car Wizard | YouTube While many trucks wearing different badges are not even on the road anymore, having been either scrapped or left to rot in junkyards, this one hasn’t suffered any catastrophic failure over all these years. It still has the original engine and transmission.\nAt exactly 252,919 miles (407,030 kilometers) and after 33 years on the road, even the bulletproof Toyota Truck is battling corrosion, despite spending its entire life in New Mexico. The corrosion might be the result of some rough off-roading and riverbed crossing.\nA pinion seal is leaking even though the owner has recently gotten it replaced. The Wizard told him to take it back to where he got it repaired and have it fixed again for no extra cost, because it should be covered by warranty.\nAnother major issue the truck came with is the old tires, which show dangerous cracks. They are date-coded 2001. That explains it! They need to be replaced ASAP, especially because the owner is planning to go on a road trip with his Toyota Truck, even though he has only approved the tune-up and the timing belt fixes.\nThe owner is well aware that the cost of repairs might be more than the actual cost of the truck. The mechanic told him he could sell it for as much as $10,000, while the repairs would cost less than half of that.\nPhoto: Car Wizard | YouTube The Car Wizard says the investment is definitely worth it because once it gets fully repaired, the good, old Toyota Truck can easily hit 750,000 miles (1,207,008 kilometers). \" This is one of the best trucks that you could buy on planet Earth, \" he says.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n1993 Toyota Pickup 1993 Toyota Truck toyota truck Toyota Hilux toyota engine\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1969 Dodge Coronet Charger SuperbeeRd Is a Shocker That'll Make You Weep, or Throw Cash at It",
